WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
The secret of the Talmud is in the Surat al-Isfah, the structure of the Suvya.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:19.000
And once I know the structure of the Suvya, all commentaries, including the Tassir commentaries, are secondary.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.000
So we are going to study the first Suvya of the third chapter of the Miracle.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:37.000
I know that we did the whole chapter, but somehow we missed the first Suvya.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
I don't know if it was on tape or something, but the tape was lost.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
So come on, you read, and it's going to be fun.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Mishnah?

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Yeah, of course.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Now, here is the strategy of fantasticism.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.000
How do you explain Kovid-Rosh?

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Do you define Kovid-Rosh?

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
It's very important, the question.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
We know what is Kalut-Rosh.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.000
We know this, so we don't even have to say it.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.000
So we know the negative expression, what Kovid-Rosh is not.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.000
It is not Kalut-Rosh.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.000
What is it?

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
The Mishnah goes into something.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Go ahead.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Yes?

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Now, the Mishnah still does not explain what is Kovid-Rosh.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000
But it tells you something about Hassidim, visually.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Just something about Hassidim.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.000
In my Holy Lenten Society, I explain what Hassidim is not.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Hassidim is not somebody who does more than the Ba'ait.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Yes?

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.000
God is Hassid bikhul ma'asar.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Yes?

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.000
So what does it mean that God is Hassid?

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Yes?

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Yes?

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.000
God is Hassid bikhul ma'asar.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Does he do more than the Ba'ait?

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Is it some stipulation?

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.000
The idea that Hassidim is more than what is required is an invention.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.000
I brought, I discussed it.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Whatever the rationality to say that Hassidim is more than what is required,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
that rationality is not found in the Talmud.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.000
It's an invention.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Hassid, in Biblical Hebrew, and so on in the Talmud,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.000
is somebody who is subtle about the needs of the other.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:54.000
In other words, has a special comprehension of what the other needs.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Is somebody who is very attentive.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Yes?

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Towards the others.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:09.000
So Hassid is a person who has this nuance in sensitivity.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
And if you are Hassid, yes?

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Like God, Hassid Imad.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.000
Imad in the sense that he is very subtle.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Yes?

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.000
In the way he analyzes people and he pleases you.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.000
What only you know.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.000
I mean, when he asks, Hassid Imad is very attentive,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
very peculiar about the needs of the other.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.000
That is Borea Olam.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.000
So Hassid is somebody who is peculiar about the needs of the other.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.000
He doesn't look into another person in a general way.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.000
He focuses into the most subtle aspects of the other person.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:05.000
So Hassid is a person who looks at things and analyzes them, again, in a meticulous way.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Alright?

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.000
So now, Hassidim Rishalim.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Why this Hassidim Rishalim?

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Because this Hassidim Rishalim, of course, we know from the time of the war we are against,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
in the Maccabean times they were called Hassidim and so on.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.000
They proved themselves.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Yes?

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.000
There is a history about this Hassidim.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.000
And by saying Hassidim Rishalim,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:34.000
it's like we have to separate between the Hassidim in post-destruction of the temple

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.000
that were problems with religiosity.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
So I think that Hassidim Rishalim is a very important point.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.000
I love the Basuk.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.000
What does Hassidim Rishalim mean?

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.000
You know, a young Kalam.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.000
And she cares.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.000
She knows about the specific requirements of her husband and so on.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:03.000
And she does everything to please the very delicate thing.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.000
So Hassidim Rishalim.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.000
Oh yes?

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Are you showing us your heart?

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.000
What does Rishalim mean?

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.000
It's a very difficult term.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.000
What does Rishalim mean?

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Show me your heart.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:21.000
Yes?

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.000
This is another big word.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.000
And I love this word.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.000
In Arabic it's inkita.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:34.000
It means you cut yourself from the world surrounding you.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:48.000
And in a sense, the world around us, yes, prevents us to be alone.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:55.000
We hear, we smell, we look at the colors, we remember things.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:00.000
And so our mind is full.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.000
Yes?

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:10.000
And we don't have the, what I call in my book, the blank space to hear the voice of God.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:22.000
And this week, when God appears in a contradiction, they call the Mama Baka.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.000
Call means voice.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.000
The Mama is silent.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.000
A silent voice.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.000
It's very interesting.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.000
Yes?

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.000
There's the paradox.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.000
The voice of God speaks and creates a silence.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.000
You know?

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.000
The presence of the voice of God creates a blank.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Yes?

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Just like in Sefer Torah you have to have a blank, otherwise you cannot write in Sefer Torah.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.000
Yes?

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.000
So it creates a blank.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Yes?

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Call the Mama.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.000
The Mama is silent.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Yes?

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.000
Yes.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Alright?

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.000
So it's called the Mama.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.000
You know?

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:02.000
It's very, very special kind of silence.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.000
Of course in that kind of silence, your mind works.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.000
You need the space, the blank space.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.000
Without the blank space of the part of the Sefer Torah, you cannot write anything.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Alright?

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.000
So what happens is...

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.000
Daka means therefore what?

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.000
That could penetrate everything.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:30.000
This is a word in physics I was trying to do.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.000
When something is permeates, that permeates everything.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.000
Alright?

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.000
And I called...

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.000
So beautiful.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.000
But when God's talking, it's a knife.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.000
Yes?

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.000
The Malkiashayet is silent.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.000
The birds are silent.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.000
The birds...

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.000
The bees are silent.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:54.000
The voice of God creates silence.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.000
In this case, in the Teflon, you are a person of God.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.000
And you have to...

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.000
Yes?

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.000
Now, I am...

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:08.000
In my Oredata society, I have a long appendix in which I say...

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:10.000
And this is true.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.000
That the Palushim...

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.000
They were the people who were...

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.000
From this world.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.000
Yes?

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.000
And...

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.000
In other words...

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.000
Pizza was not that important.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.000
Am I right?

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.000
You have to eat pizza, you eat pizza.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.000
But...

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:34.000
You have to be able to cut yourself.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.000
You know?

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:38.000
From...

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.000
From this.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.000
You know?

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.000
I mean...

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.000
You have to have the ability of cutting, of showing.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.000
This is fantastic when I say interval.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.000
Yes?

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:50.000
You have to show in.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.000
In interval form.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.000
This is...

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.000
Stop.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.000
I want to get off now.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.000
All right?

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.000
So...

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.000
And then...

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.000
All right?

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.000
I don't want to go too much.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:06.000
I think I discussed it also in the same appendix in the Oredata society.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.000
Finally, I think I know what...

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.000
The word holy.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.000
You have the name holy.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.000
Yes?

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.000
And...

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.000
And...

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.000
And...

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.000
And...

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:18.000
And...

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.000
Actually...

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:23.000
It's another big word, which is actually in Hebrew.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.000
Adonai Moshe Kela'em.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.000
Means put him in a jail.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.000
Yes?

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Put him in a jail.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:32.000
Kela'em.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.000
A better care, you say, now in modern Hebrew.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:42.000
And holy is someone who makes himself in a kind of a prison.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.000
He knows how to isolate himself.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.000
And to, you know...

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.000
That's what it means.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.000
He knows how to imprison himself.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.000
And I promised not to speak about this.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:00.000
Sometimes you have certain weaknesses.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.000
And then you create a kind of a prison.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.000
In the word, I think...

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:10.000
It's not used in Hebrew or Spanish.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.000
But it's used in modern Spanish.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.000
Jaula.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.000
Jaula is a place where you put a bird or something.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.000
A cage.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.000
Yes?

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.000
And I think that jaula in Spanish...

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:25.000
In the Jewish-Spanish, we don't have that.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.000
I didn't mean it in the Jewish-Spanish.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:33.000
But in Spanish, my Jewish-Spanish, jaula is a cage.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:37.000
And that's exactly what holy is.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:40.000
It's somebody...

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.000
I remember there was a man, a wonderful man.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.000
And he asked me once...

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.000
What is molcho? Is haq molcho?

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.000
No, not haq molcho.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:53.000
The father of the...

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:58.000
The molcho, which is the father of the Rehkanati band.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.000
A very intelligent man.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.000
From Saloniki.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.000
And he asked me what holy means.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.000
And I didn't know.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:07.000
Yes?

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.000
I didn't know also.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.000
Alright.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:12.000
But after three years, I figured out what it is.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.000
So...

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:19.000
So again, we don't know exactly what is...

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
We will know eventually.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.000
How about one more sentence?

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.000
This is a hyperbola.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.000
But this shows the intensity.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.000
Of course, the haraka is if you...

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.000
Now, I explain...

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.000
Not I'm your haker.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.000
But it's a haker.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.000
Of course, it's not...

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.000
This haker is not going to attack you.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:58.000
Yes?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.000
You're scared.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.000
But...

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:07.000
I explain it doesn't mean I'm the haker of the person.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.000
I'm not the haker of the...

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.000
Alright?

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:15.000
Yes.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:16.000
Yes.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.000
And therefore, it is...

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.000
The haraka is that it's a hyperbola.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:23.000
Yes.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:24.000
Alright?

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:26.000
Just to...

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.000
Again, we want to have an idea of what does mean COVID loss.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.000
Yes?

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.000
But we still, we don't know.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:33.000
But...

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.000
Now, we're going to have a fantastic story.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:40.000
Try to explain what COVID loss is.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:41.000
How about?

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:44.000
Yes.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.000
Now, as soon as we know...

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:50.000
As soon as we ask...

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:53.000
You know that the question is...

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.000
We are asking for some sort of collaboration.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:57.000
Yes?

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.000
It's not...

00:12:58.000 --> 00:12:59.000
It's not the alright time.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.000
It's not a question of the right time.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:02.000
It's not a question...

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:03.000
It's not...

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.000
It's not natural to say COVID loss.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.000
Yes.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:12.000
Can you give me some sort of collaboration?

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.000
No, always.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.000
Whatever it is, it's...

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:23.000
No.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:24.000
Because...

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:28.000
But the proof is not the right time.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.000
Do you understand?

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.000
Always.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:32.000
Maybe we have Kabbalah.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:33.000
You know that it's the right time.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:34.000
Whatever it is.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:35.000
And...

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:36.000
But it...

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:37.000
But it's not...

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.000
But it's not the right time.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.000
Yes?

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:47.000
It's collaboration.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:49.000
No.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:50.000
No, no.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:51.000
It's a difference.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.000
It's a difference between...

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:53.000
People don't know.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.000
There's a difference between a proof and a collaboration.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:00.000
You could prove something in chemistry and physics,

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.000
and you show the mathematics,

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.000
and you show the relationship between A and B.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:05.000
To corroborate,

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.000
it means I have a thesis,

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.000
and the audience says,

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:14.000
people that were born under the star X,

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:15.000
they're going to be...

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.000
I could corroborate this by pointing them this,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.000
but also there are exceptions, you know.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.000
And therefore, this is a corroboration.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:24.000
It's not a proof.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:25.000
Yes?

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.000
You know it's true from another place.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:29.000
What?

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.000
You already know it's true from a different place.

